\section{Purpose of the Document Class Declaration}

The document class declaration is the foundational command of any \LaTeX{} file.
It defines the global layout, structural hierarchy, and formatting rules applied
throughout the document. This command must appear as the first instruction in
the source file.

\section{Syntax}

The general syntax is:
\begin{verbatim}
\documentclass[options]{class}
\end{verbatim}

\subsection*{Class Options}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Font size:} 10pt, 11pt, 12pt
    \item \textbf{Paper size:} a4paper, letterpaper
    \item \textbf{Layout:} oneside, twoside, draft
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Common Classes}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{article} — journal papers, white sheets, reports
    \item \texttt{report} — multi-chapter documents, theses
    \item \texttt{book} — full-length books
    \item \texttt{letter} — correspondence
    \item \texttt{beamer} — presentations
    \item \texttt{revtex4-2} — APS journal submissions
\end{itemize}

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Standard Example}

A typical production-grade declaration:
\begin{verbatim}
\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}

\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}
\usepackage{geometry}
\geometry{margin=1in}

\begin{document}
\title{\textbf{Document Title}}
\author{C. Matthew Blackwell}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Your document body text begins here...

\end{document}
\end{verbatim}

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Summary}

The document class declaration determines the global structure of your \LaTeX{}
file. Selecting the correct class and options ensures that the document compiles
with the intended formatting, layout, and professional standards.

